      <h1>CrossPoint 2.0 vs KOReader: Open E-Reader Firmware Compared</h1>
      <p class="meta"><time datetime="2026-11-19">Published November 19, 2026</time></p>

      <p>Two projects are quietly reshaping how you read EPUB files on hardware you own. <a href="https://github.com/koreader/koreader">KOReader</a> is the mature open-source reader engine — 31,193 GitHub stars, AGPL-licensed, written in Lua, running on Kindle, Kobo, PocketBook, reMarkable, Cervantes, Android and Linux desktops. <a href="https://crosspointreader.com">CrossPoint Reader</a> is the newer arrival: MIT-licensed C++ firmware built specifically for small ESP32-based e-readers, and as of November 1, 2026 it ships a plugin architecture that is structurally unlike anything KOReader has done.</p>

      <p>CrossPoint 2.0 is the most substantial release the project has produced. Its GitHub release page lists 192 merged pull requests, 28 of them first-time contributions, and the release spans 206 commits from 44 distinct authors. A Hacker News thread for the project on November 18, 2026 drew 120 points and 58 comments — the kind of attention that usually means a tool is crossing from niche hobby to wider attention.</p>

      <p>This is a comparison of the two as they stand in November 2026. It matters for anyone who has a shelf of EPUB files and wants to know where open reader software is heading, and it matters more concretely for anyone choosing between flashing CrossPoint onto a small e-reader or running KOReader on a device that already ships with it.</p>

      <h2>What CrossPoint Reader actually is</h2>

      <p>CrossPoint is not an app. It is firmware — the operating system that runs an e-reader. It began as community firmware for the Xteink X3 and X4 pocket devices and has since broadened into a platform. The project's own scope document defines the mission as "an efficient, open-source reading experience for ESP32-based e-reader devices," with a guiding principle worth quoting because it explains most of the design decisions: the firmware exists "to do the things the stock firmware does poorly or not at all."</p>

      <p>That principle has consequences. CrossPoint's scope document explicitly rejects several things other readers support, and the list is instructive:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>No PDF rendering.</strong> PDFs are fixed-layout, so rendering them means panning and zooming, which is a poor experience on e-ink. KOReader, by contrast, supports PDF and DjVu natively and can reflow scanned documents through the bundled K2pdfopt library.</li>
        <li><strong>No active connectivity.</strong> No RSS readers, news aggregators or web browsers. Background Wi-Fi drains the battery, and the ESP32's single core makes it impractical.</li>
        <li><strong>No writing or authoring tools.</strong> No notepads, journals or editors — the input hardware and RAM are wrong for it.</li>
        <li><strong>No interactive apps.</strong> No calculators, no games.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>CrossPoint 2.0 also has a <a href="https://github.com/crosspoint-reader/crosspoint-reader/blob/develop/SCOPE.md">temporarily frozen surface</a>: new themes, and new external network connectors such as sync engines, cloud storage clients and remote file access. Existing OPDS support can be improved; entirely new protocols cannot be added until that freeze lifts.</p>

      <p>Version 2.0 shipped on November 1, 2026 with prebuilt binaries for eight device targets: the Xteink X3 and X4, the X4 Classic, the X4 Pro, the Eegoread A4, the Metalio E-Ink 4, the M5Stack PaperMono, the BOOX Picco and the reTerminal Sticky. Four of those — Metalio, Eegoread, and BOOX Picco in both standard and Lite form — were new in this release, which is the clearest signal of the multi-device push described in the project's roadmap.</p>

      <h2>The plugin system: the real story of 2.0</h2>

      <p>If you take one thing away from CrossPoint 2.0, take this. The headline features are tidy: Bluetooth page turners now work on all devices, you can highlight passages and save clippings, OPDS 2.0 catalogs are supported, line spacing has seven granular steps instead of three presets, and a black-and-white dithering option avoids a full screen refresh on image pages.</p>

      <p>But the architectural change is the <strong>SD-card plugin system</strong>, and it is the part no outlet covered and no competing firmware has replicated.</p>

      <p>The design principle, stated in the project's plugin documentation, is that "CrossPoint keeps service integrations off the firmware." A plugin is just a folder on the SD card. The firmware exposes four generic, scheme-neutral surfaces, and the plugin composes whichever ones it needs:</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Browser plugins (<code>plugin.js</code>).</strong> JavaScript that runs in your browser tab — not on the device — backed by generic device endpoints such as <code>/api/relay</code>, <code>/api/crypto</code>, <code>/api/fetch</code> and <code>/api/plugin-fs</code>. This is where anything arbitrary lives: request signing, HTML-to-EPUB conversion, RSS-to-EPUB feeds. The catch is that the browser page must stay open while it works.</li>
        <li><strong>The plugin job queue.</strong> External systems can trigger plugin actions without a human clicking anything. The firmware stores opaque <code>&#123;plugin, action, args&#125;</code> JSON blobs in a fixed six-slot pool that it never interprets; an open page — including a headless runner at <code>/plugins-run</code> — claims jobs, executes the handler, and posts the result back.</li>
        <li><strong>On-device manifests (<code>device.json</code>).</strong> A declarative manifest the firmware renders natively: sign in, browse, download, attach metadata. Enough for most book services, with no code running on the device at all.</li>
        <li><strong>Firmware events and the book metadata sidecar.</strong> The firmware can record whitelisted moments — a reading session, sleep entry, a finished download — into a per-plugin queue and replay them through HTTP request templates declared in the manifest.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>The consequence is that adding a new book service no longer requires a firmware build, and the firmware itself carries no vendor names, URLs or file-format knowledge. The project also states the limits plainly: plugins cannot replace the reading engine, install hardware drivers or add unsupported document formats, and will not become a platform for games, calculators or PDF rendering.</p>

      <h2>KOReader's plugin model, by contrast</h2>

      <p>KOReader's extension system is a different philosophy entirely, and understanding the contrast is the point of this comparison. KOReader plugins are <strong>real programs running on the device</strong>, written in Lua, shipped as <code>.koplugin</code> directories, and loaded by a plugin loader that runs inside the reader's own process. The repository currently ships 37 of them, and they are genuinely powerful:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><code>newsdownloader</code> — fetch RSS feeds and convert articles to EPUB on the device itself</li>
        <li><code>exporter</code> — send highlights and notes to Joplin, Readwise, Nextcloud, Obsidian-style Markdown targets, plain text, HTML or JSON</li>
        <li><code>cloudstorage</code> — mount Dropbox, WebDAV and FTP servers as libraries</li>
        <li><code>terminal</code> — an actual shell on the reader, with a working prompt</li>
        <li><code>opds</code>, <code>calibre</code>, <code>kosync</code>, <code>vocabbuilder</code>, <code>japanese</code> and roughly thirty more</li>
      </ul>

      <p>KOReader's on-device Lua model is more capable than CrossPoint's declarative manifest in one direction — it can do real logic on the device — and weaker in another. It ties the feature to the firmware's Lua runtime and the device's CPU budget, and it means a plugin author must work inside the reader's own process. CrossPoint's model deliberately moves the heavy logic to a browser you already own and gives the device a small, bounded vocabulary instead.</p>

      <p>This is a genuine trade-off, not a winner-takes-all comparison. If you want an RSS pipeline that turns articles into EPUBs while you are away from your laptop, KOReader's <code>newsdownloader</code> does it today with no browser involved. If you want a signing-based service integration that would be dangerous to ship as on-device code, CrossPoint's browser surface is the safer place for it.</p>

      <h2>Format support, and where each one stops</h2>

      <p>Format coverage is where the two projects differ most sharply, and it is the single most important practical consideration if you own a mixed library.</p>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Capability</th><th>CrossPoint Reader</th><th>KOReader</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>EPUB 2 and 3</td><td>Core format; EPUB optimizer on-device</td><td>Core format</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Reflowable extras</td><td>TXT and Markdown via the EPUB pipeline</td><td>EPUB, FB2, MOBI, DOC, RTF, HTML, CHM, TXT</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fixed-layout</td><td>Not supported — explicitly out of scope</td><td>PDF, DjVu, CBT, CBZ</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Scanned PDF reflow</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, via bundled K2pdfopt</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Catalogs</td><td>OPDS 1.x and OPDS 2.0</td><td>OPDS, plus Calibre and Wallabag integration</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Library transfer</td><td>Browser upload, WebDAV, Wi-Fi hotspot, Calibre wireless</td><td>Calibre wireless, OPDS, Wi-Fi transfer</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Highlights and notes</td><td>Clippings, sync via CrossPoint Sync, Kindle-format <code>My Clippings.txt</code> export</td><td>Highlights, notes, and a broad export plugin set</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cross-device sync</td><td>KOSync-compatible</td><td>KOSync-native</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Offline dictionaries</td><td>StarDict, multi-word phrase lookup</td><td>StarDict, bundled hyphenation dictionaries</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>Two details in that table are worth pulling out. First, CrossPoint 2.0 unified TXT and Markdown rendering through the EPUB pipeline — a refactor that means plain text and Markdown files are now typeset by the same engine that handles books, so they get justified text, hyphenation and the same font handling. Second, CrossPoint's clipping export writes up to 2,000 UTF-8 bytes of each excerpt to a <code>My Clippings.txt</code> file in the SD card root using Kindle-compatible formatting, which means highlights survive a move to an older Kindle workflow in a way that is easy to overlook.</p>

      <h2>The numbers behind the projects</h2>

      <p>All figures below were pulled directly from the GitHub API and the project sites on November 19, 2026, rather than from outlet coverage.</p>

