      <p>One caveat worth flagging: CrossPoint's own website carries a set of headline counters, and they do not all agree with the repository. The site claims "242 contributors around the world" and "126 changes in the last major release," while the GitHub API returns 283 contributors and the 2.0.0 release notes list 192 merged pull requests. The site's fork figure — 1,947 "community forks" — is also a different measurement from GitHub's 2,372 total forks for the repository, since GitHub counts every fork of every fork. Use the API numbers if you plan to cite them.</p>

      <p>CrossPoint's project site also claims 281,736 devices running the firmware. There is no public API that substantiates that figure, so treat it as a project claim rather than a measured fact.</p>

      <h2>What the 2.0 release actually changed for readers</h2>

      <p>Beyond the plugin architecture, several 2.0 changes affect day-to-day reading:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Typography.</strong> The renderer now handles drop caps, small caps, mixed text sizes, borders and shaded blocks. Tables take column content, cell padding and borders into account, and headings stay attached to the text that follows them. Justified text is the default, with language-aware hyphenation and working footnotes.</li>
        <li><strong>Line spacing.</strong> Seven options from 0% to +75% added spacing replaced the old Tight/Normal/Wide presets.</li>
        <li><strong>Fonts.</strong> Noto Serif moved from a built-in font to an optional SD card font to free up flash. If you use it, install <code>NotoSerif</code> through Manage Fonts before or after upgrading, or a Noto Serif selection falls back to Noto Sans.</li>
        <li><strong>Bluetooth page turners.</strong> Available on all devices, with short and long presses mapped to page turns, chapter navigation, the reader menu or clipping selection.</li>
        <li><strong>Images.</strong> Non-interlaced GIF images render, covers included. A new black-and-white dithering option avoids the full refresh on image pages.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Note the deliberate trade-off in the font change: the built-in Noto Serif was moved out to SD card to make room for all of the above. That is a flash-budget decision made in favour of more features, and it means an upgrade is not entirely transparent for anyone relying on that specific face.</p>

      <h2>The honest caveats</h2>

      <p>CrossPoint is one year old as a repository, and the comparison has real asymmetries worth stating plainly.</p>

      <p><strong>Hardware reach is the biggest gap.</strong> KOReader runs today on Kindle, Kobo, PocketBook, reMarkable, Cervantes, Android and desktop Linux. CrossPoint runs on ESP32-based small e-readers — a much narrower category, though one where it is genuinely excellent. If you already own a Kindle or Kobo and it has never been jailbroken, KOReader is available to you today and CrossPoint is not.</p>

      <p><strong>Format coverage is narrower.</strong> If your library contains MOBI, FB2, RTF, CHM, DJVU, CBZ or PDF files — or scanned PDFs you want reflowed — KOReader handles them and CrossPoint does not. This matters enormously for people migrating a decades-old Kindle library, a topic we covered in our <a href="/blog/migrate-kindle-library-to-epub/">Kindle library migration guide</a> and in <a href="/blog/epub-vs-pdf-vs-mobi-vs-azw3/">the EPUB vs PDF vs MOBI vs AZW3 comparison</a>.</p>

      <p><strong>Some devices ship a modified CrossPoint.</strong> We reported separately that the BOOX Picco ships an undisclosed fork of CrossPoint firmware, which Onyx does not publicly recommend flashing over. That situation is unchanged by 2.0, and it is worth remembering when comparing a "CrossPoint device" to a "CrossPoint device".</p>

      <p><strong>Upstream supply-chain caution.</strong> The November 18 Hacker News thread surfaced a recurring complaint from Xteink buyers in China: the Chinese-market firmware locks direct USB flashing of the ESP32, which is defensible as a way to protect against mid-refresh corruption but leaves a faulty bootloader with no recovery path. Units bought through the vendor's own store or Amazon are generally unlocked; units from marketplace sellers may not be. Verify the flash path before you commit to a device.</p>

      <h2>Which one should you pick?</h2>

      <p>If you have a device that KOReader already supports — any Kindle, Kobo, PocketBook or reMarkable — KOReader remains the more capable and more broadly useful choice today. It has the larger install base, the deeper plugin ecosystem, the wider format support and a mature sync story. Nothing in CrossPoint 2.0 changes that today.</p>

      <p>CrossPoint 2.0 wins in a narrower but real set of situations: you own or plan to own a small ESP32 e-reader such as an Xteink X4, a BOOX Picco or a PaperMono, and you value a distraction-free reading experience, a plugin architecture that keeps third-party service code off the device, and a firmware that ships new device support on a monthly cadence rather than annually.</p>

      <p>The most interesting thing about this comparison is not that either project is winning. It is that the two are pulling in opposite directions on purpose. KOReader extends the device — more formats, more on-device apps, more logic running where the reader is. CrossPoint constrains the device — a smaller, sharper reading experience with a plugin model that pushes complexity into a browser you already own. The next few years of open e-reading will probably contain both, because they solve for different hardware and different people.</p>

