        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">What is CrossPoint Reader and how is it different from KOReader?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>CrossPoint Reader is open-source MIT-licensed firmware written in C++ that runs on small ESP32-based e-readers such as the Xteink X3/X4 and the BOOX Picco. KOReader is AGPL-3.0 reader software written in Lua that runs on existing Kindle, Kobo, PocketBook and reMarkable devices. CrossPoint replaces the device's entire operating system; KOReader runs alongside whatever firmware the device already has.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">Can KOReader read EPUB files that CrossPoint cannot open?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>Yes, and it is the most important practical difference. KOReader supports EPUB, FB2, MOBI, DOC, RTF, HTML, CHM and TXT as reflowable formats, plus PDF, DjVu, CBT and CBZ as fixed-layout, and it can reflow scanned documents using the bundled K2pdfopt library. CrossPoint is EPUB-first and explicitly excludes PDF rendering as out of scope; it renders TXT and Markdown through the EPUB pipeline. If your library contains MOBI or PDF, KOReader is currently the safer choice.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">What is the SD-card plugin system in CrossPoint 2.0?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>It is a plugin architecture where integrations live in a folder on the SD card rather than in the firmware, so adding or updating one never requires a firmware build. The firmware exposes four generic surfaces: browser plugins written in JavaScript, a six-slot job queue for external automation, declarative on-device catalog manifests, and a firmware event queue with a book metadata sidecar. Plugins cannot replace the reading engine, add hardware drivers or introduce unsupported document formats.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">Is KOReader's Lua plugin system better than CrossPoint's?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>It depends on what the plugin needs to do. KOReader's 37 Lua plugins run inside the reader's own process, so they can do real logic on the device — its newsdownloader plugin turns RSS feeds into EPUBs offline, and its terminal plugin gives you a shell. CrossPoint's model deliberately moves arbitrary logic into a browser on your phone or computer, which is more capable for things like request signing but requires the browser page to stay open. CrossPoint also supports OPDS 2.0, which KOReader's OPDS plugin does not.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">How do I install CrossPoint 2.0 on an e-reader?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>CrossPoint publishes prebuilt binaries for eight targets on its GitHub releases page, including the Xteink X3 and X4, X4 Classic, X4 Pro, Eegoread A4, Metalio E-Ink 4, M5Stack PaperMono, BOOX Picco and reTerminal Sticky. Supported devices can update over the air via Wi-Fi; BOOX Picco updates use USB. Before flashing, verify that your unit has an unlocked flash path — some Chinese-market devices lock direct USB flashing, which leaves no recovery path if a faulty bootloader is installed.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">Does upgrading to CrossPoint 2.0 require reinstalling fonts?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>Only one font. Noto Serif moved from a built-in font to an optional SD card font to free up flash space for the new features. Install it through Manage Fonts before or after upgrading, otherwise a Noto Serif selection falls back to Noto Sans. The Libron font is also available for direct download from the font manager.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
